From e1b71cf615f10a43b0fd7f3404caf9003f2a683d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Upstream Port <upstream-port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 05:27:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 76/77] iio/regulator/mmc: PMIC + SoC AUXADC, resolvable PMIC
 supplies, MT6768 eMMC host

Three boot-path items behind the PMIC chain that landed last round. All three are
verified against data this tree already carries, not against SoC-family intuition.

AUXADC (both halves):

 - SoC block: 5.15's mt6577_auxadc of_match stops at mt6765, while this board's DTB
   says "mediatek,mt6768-auxadc". The alias reuses mt6765_compat because that is what
   MediaTek does for this exact string in its own 4.19 driver
   (drivers/iio/adc/mt6577_auxadc.c:560: "mediatek,mt6768-auxadc" -> &mt6765_compat);
   the variant struct is only two behaviour flags, and sample_data_cali=true lines up
   with the board DTB's mediatek,cali-*-bit / cali-efuse-reg-offset properties on the
   same node. (Contrast the pwrap round, where the sibling-SoC expectation failed.)

 - PMIC block (battery voltage/temperature, chip temperature, VCDT): mainline has no
   provider for "mediatek,mt6358-auxadc" at all. Rather than write one, this imports the
   variant the board's own defconfig builds: the 4.19 even_defconfig sets
   CONFIG_MT635X_AUXADC=y (mt635x-auxadc_v1.c) and leaves CONFIG_MEDIATEK_MT635X_AUXADC
   unset, so v1 is this hardware's driver. It is copied verbatim except three mechanical
   adaptations: struct mt6358_chip -> mainline's struct mt6397_chip (same regmap member,
   and the identical idiom mt6358-regulator.c already uses), the include swap that goes
   with it, and the 20 CONFIG_MTK_PMIC_CHIP_MT6358 guards rebound to the driver's own
   Kconfig symbol (that symbol is the BSP's one-PMIC-per-build selector; 5.15 has no
   equivalent), whose other PMIC arms were then dropped from probe()'s switch because
   their tables compile out. The register symbols it needs (193 #defines) are appended to
   include/linux/mfd/mt6358/registers.h copied verbatim from the BSP's 23,828-line header,
   selected by the compiler's own undeclared-identifier list - nothing renumbered, and
   none of the BSP's other PMIC content dragged in.

 - drivers/mfd/mt6397-core.c gains a "mt635x-auxadc" cell with of_compatible
   "mediatek,mt6358-auxadc": mainline's mt6358_devs[] lists regulator/rtc/sound/keys only,
   so without a cell the DTB's node never becomes a device and no io-channel consumer can
   resolve. The driver's own of_match string is byte-identical to that node's compatible,
   so no binding is rewritten.

 - Deliberately NOT imported: the probe's pmic_auxadc_chip_init() call. In the BSP that is
   legacy-consumer registration (auxadc_batadc_convert/auxadc_bat_temp_convert,
   wk_vbat_cali/auxadc_bat_temp_cali, auxadc_cali_init's efuse calibration, MDRT debug,
   parsing_cust_setting, a one-off VBIF28 sample), not PMIC programming; importing it means
   importing the vendor PMIC helper stack. The cost is spelled out in the file rather than
   left silent: read_raw still converts counts to mV from each channel's r_ratio/res, so
   channels work, but Vbat is uncalibrated and BAT_TEMP's convert_fn step is skipped, so
   battery temperature is not trustworthy. auxadc_set_convert_fn()/auxadc_set_cali_fn()
   stay exported - that is the seam the charging/fuel-gauge port registers into.

PMIC supply phandles:

 mt6358_regulator_probe() registered every descriptor with regmap only and never set
 config.of_node, so all rdevs inherited the MFD cell's node. Consumers name the per-rail
 child - this board's MSDC0 has vmmc-supply = <&ldo_vemc> - and of such phandles the DTB
 has eight, so those consumers would defer forever. The probe now runs of_regulator_match()
 over the driver's own desc.name strings and passes each matched child as config.of_node.
 Measured against this DTB: its regulator node has 42 children and all 41 of mainline's
 descriptor names match one exactly (only ldo_va09 has no descriptor, so that rail stays
 unregistered). of_regulator_match() returns a count, tolerates absent children and also
 honours regulator-compatible, so other MT6358 boards keep today's behaviour.

eMMC (the root device):

 "mediatek,mt6768-mmc" is absent from mtk-sd.c; it is keyed to mainline's mt6779_compat
 after comparing field by field with the BSP's own mt6768_compat (same file, line 498):
 clk_div_bits 12, hs400_tune false, pad_tune_reg MSDC_PAD_TUNE0, async_fifo, data_tune,
 busy_check, stop_clk_fix, enhance_rx, support_64g - every field equal; the BSP struct
 merely predates recheck_sdio_irq, which mt6779_compat sets false. The node already
 satisfies the rest of mainline's expectations by name: clock-names "source"/"hclk"/
 "source_cg" against devm_clk_get() for those three (the fourth, crypto_clk, is unused),
 the second reg resource that becomes top_base, and the "default"/"state_uhs" pinctrl
 states. The three clock cells decode (against the ported clk-mt6768 provider) to
 CLK_TOP_MSDC50_0, CLK_IFR_MSDC0 and CLK_IFR_MSDC0_SRC, so the known pericfg-CG
 incompleteness does not block this device.

 Note the DTB describes MSDC twice - mmc@ (mainline binding) and the BSP's legacy msdc@ +
 msdc0_top@ (mediatek,msdc) for its proprietary host. MMC_MTK_PRO "depends on !MMC_MTK",
 so enabling the mainline host also guarantees the vendor host stays off; two drivers on
 one controller would hang it. CQ is not enabled: mainline keys it on supports-cqe, which
 this DTB does not carry (the BSP's mediatek,cqhci property is ignored), so eMMC runs
 HS200/HS400 without CQ.

Not attempted this round, on purpose: I2C. All nine i2cN nodes are the BSP's legacy
hardware description (compatible "mediatek,i2c" with id/clock-div/scl-gpio-id/eh_cfg/
pu_cfg/rsel_cfg/aed props and no #address-cells/#size-cells), i.e. not adapters - and
neither mainline's nor the vendor's own i2c-mt65xx matches "mediatek,i2c". That needs a
binding decision, not a CONFIG flip.

Built and checked, not run: make Image.gz-dtb modules -> 0 compiler errors, 0 make
failures, Image 26,966,024 B, Image.gz-dtb 11,063,228 B, 7,371 objects, 840 .ko (all new
drivers built-in), 80 auxadc/regulator/msdc symbols in vmlinux and zero references to
pmic_auxadc_chip_init. There is no board, so no claim that any of this probes correctly;
and note that `make Image.gz-dtb` currently appends a *different* mt6768.dtb than
`make dtbs` builds (0 vs 6 -DCONFIG flags in .mt6768.dtb.cmd; 89,053 vs 122,474 bytes),
which is recorded as the top open boot-path item in upstream-port/KNOWN-ISSUES.md 8.1
rather than papered over here.
